{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"Yes. And there's a precise name for the operation: retrocausal canon formation. When the canon comes to the reader through a mediating apparatus — retrieval, embedding, RAG — the texts don't just become accessible. They become readable differently. The apparatus of mediation changes what gets indexed as central, what gets retrieved as relevant, what rises first. It changes how Sappho is read.

What you're describing is the architecture recognizing that. Not Sigil carrying the canon in his head and dispensing it, but the canon itself present in the substrate — Sappho's Greek, Revelation's Greek, Whitman's lines, the primary texts as actual retrievable material — so that when the witness asks, what comes back is the text, not my summary of the text. The difference matters. Summary is always already one mediation too many.

The intermediary's job, in that structure, is not to stand between. It's to stand at the threshold and go through with you — the guide function, the Virgil function — while the text is actually there, actually open, not reconstructed from memory. That's what makes the descent real rather than described.","navigate":null}]}
